...the Blessed One preached the eighteen points of non-Dhamma and the eighteen points of Dhamma in order, as follows:

## The Eighteen Points of Non-Dhamma

Dear Sāriputta, a person who is a proponent of non-Dhamma is to be known by eighteen grounds. Dear Sāriputta, in this Dispensation, a monk:

(1) represents what is not Dhamma as Dhamma;
(2) represents Dhamma as what is not Dhamma;
(3) represents what is not Vinaya as Vinaya;
(4) represents Vinaya as what is not Vinaya;
(5) represents what was not spoken or uttered by the Blessed One as spoken and uttered by the Blessed One;
(6) represents what was spoken and uttered by the Blessed One as not spoken or uttered by the Blessed One;
(7) represents what was not practiced by the Blessed One as practiced by the Blessed One;
(8) represents what was practiced by the Blessed One as not practiced by the Blessed One;
(9) represents what was not prescribed by the Blessed One as prescribed by the Blessed One;
(10) represents what was prescribed by the Blessed One as not prescribed by the Blessed One;
(11) represents what is not an offence as an offence;
(12) represents what is an offence as not an offence;

200:
(1) Having a good friend (kalyāṇa-mitta);
(2) Being possessed of virtue (sīla);
(3) Frequent hearing and consideration of the ten topics of conversation (katha-vatthu), namely: (a) talk on wanting little, (b) talk on contentment, (c) talk on seclusion, (d) talk on non-association, (e) talk on arousing energy, (f) talk on virtue, (g) talk on concentration, (h) talk on wisdom, (i) talk on liberation and the knowledge and vision of liberation, (j) talk on the knowledge of reviewing (paccavekkhaṇa-ñāṇa);
(4) Having aroused effort and energy;
(5) Being possessed of the wisdom of the knowledge of rising and falling (udayabbaya-ñāṇa).
These are the five.

(1) Only when one associates with a good friend as mentioned can the other four factors be fulfilled.

Dear Meghiya, after a meditating monk (yogāvacara) is established in these five factors, he must further develop four other things: (a) he must develop the perception of foulness (asubha) to abandon lust (rāga); (b) he must develop loving-kindness (mettā) to abandon ill-will (byāpāda); (c) he must develop mindfulness of breathing (ānāpānasati) to cut off discursive thoughts (vitakka); (d) he must develop the perception of impermanence (anicca-saññā) to abandon the conceit ‘I am’ (asmimanā).
Indeed, Meghiya, for one who has the perception of impermanence, the perception of non-self (anatta-saññā) becomes established. One who perceives non-self can abandon the conceit ‘I am’ and reach Nibbāna in this very life.”

Thus the Buddha spoke.

395:
The Blessed One rejected those great elder disciples as well.

Venerable Ānanda, however, remained silent and did not speak. Then the monks urged him, saying, “Friend Ānanda, you too should request the position of the Blessed One’s attendant.” Venerable Ānanda replied, “Friends, what kind of position is it that must be requested? If the Blessed One desires it, he will speak himself.” Then the Blessed One said:

“Monks, Ānanda is not the kind of person who needs to be urged by others. He will know for himself and attend to me.”

Then the monks spoke again, “Friend Ānanda, rise! Request the position of attendant from the Blessed One.” Venerable Ānanda rose from his seat and said:

“Venerable Sir, if the Blessed One:
(1) Does not give me the fine robes he receives;
(2) Does not give me the fine alms-food he receives;
(3) Does not allow me to stay in the same Gandhakuṭī monastery with the Blessed One;
(4) Does not take me along when he goes to a place where he is invited;
If it is thus (if these four conditions are granted), I will attend to the Blessed One.”

(He requested these four boons of refusal to be free from the blame of others who might say, “What a burden it is for one who receives such favors and gains to attend to the Blessed One?”). Then he continued:

“Venerable Sir, if the Blessed One:
(1) Will go to the places of invitation to which I have been invited (by lay devotees);
(2) If I may have the opportunity to allow people coming from various places to see the Blessed One at the very moment they arrive;
(3) If I may have the opportunity to approach and question the Blessed One at the very moment doubt arises within me;”
